{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1534256-how-many-of-us-read-manga-mostly","id":1534256,"title":"How many of us read Manga mostly?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-04-15T11:14:55-07:00","id":1534256,"title":"How many of us read Manga mostly?","content":"Thanks to Heavy Arms for a topic idea. So how of you have drifted away to Manga series instead of reading series from DC, Marvel, Image, Dark Horse, IDW, Boom Studios or Dynamite for whatever reasons and what do you like about Manga the most?<br /> <br /> Myself I like the wide array of genres that are out there and found some good reads like Naruto, Spy Family, Ice Blade Sorceror just to name a few.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T05:37:06-07:00","id":1534294,"title":"","content":"For me, there are periods where I read a lot of Manga and/or Comics and then lengthy periods where I read none. Especially the main-ones, Marvel and DC just get tiresome after a while. With Mangas the problem is that it really has to pique my interest before I commit to reading the thing. There's so much generic shit in the Manga-market. The stereotypes are different, of course, compared to Western comics, but they're stereotypes all the same. Oh, sure, yet another stupid isekai about some idiot who couldn't hack it in the real world and then assembles a harem in a fantasy-world because he never loses a fight for whatever reason... There are way too many of those! Not that Western comics are any better with stereotypes. But<br /> <br /> The mangas I'm currently reading are Chainsaw Man and Sousou no Frieren. Both because the animes were great and actually piqued my interest in picking up the mangas to read more. I enjoyed the second season of Jujutsu Kaisen but you won't get me to read a Shounen Jump series in manga-format week-to-week. I'm fine with waiting for the next anime-season for a series like that. These Shounen-series have some serious pacing problems.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale","name":"Knightingale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27535_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T11:58:04-07:00","id":1534298,"title":"","content":"As a bit of clarification, I personally didn't drift to manga/etc. away from American comics specifically. I drifted away from American superhero comics as the major publishers have been in an atrocious downward spiral since the late 90s born of constantly falling back on the status quo instead of innovation (including the unpredictable first few years of doing new things before you really know if the audience is sticking with it). I still read, and support (even if &quot;buying&quot; isn't always how things work in the current era) a large number of independent Western comic creators, I haven buy the occasional thing from some of the bigger companies if it appeals (Image is generally better at supporting series that are going to end, so they don't spiral into endless reboots).<br /> <br /> I started reading manga while I was still in my American superhero comics phase. It was never an either/or thing for me. Manga certainly took over for a bit because as an American youth there was a giant library of things to explore that were new and unfamiliar, and the whole different approach to publications meant different types of stories and themes. But as I both came to understand that the manga industry has plenty of its own corporate problems, and had its own ways of recycling the same-old same-old, and cliched tropes,and so on, as well as having access to global independent comic artists out there thanks to the Internet (though plenty of problems finding ones you like because that's a different beast), manga didn't just take over my entertainment habits.<br /> <br /> These days I would say online independent comics make up the majority of comic reading without regard for whether you would classify them in any given genre or cultural style.<br /> <br /> The thing I like about manga the most, as an American, is that along with anime, it's drastically shifted the culture into accepting that comics and animation are capable of telling as many different stories as any other medium. Like, I don't think we would have seen the success of The Walking Dead franchise (as much as the shows have outstayed their welcome and the video games are very hit or miss) if Image wasn't feeling the pressure to start competing in more genres thanks to comic-kid turning into comic-adults and finding that manga was far more accessible to their changing tastes than mainstream Western comics had been for decades. Kirkman himself has said that Image initially rejected TWD because they felt zombies were too played out and they wanted something with more flair.. so he lied and said that TWD was going to have a massive twist and turn into an alien invasion story later in the plot to get them on board with the project. By the time it was clear the aliens were never showing up, TWD was successful enough by just being a good zombie comic that Image didn't care. But TWD happened after there was an acceptance of mainstream high quality comic books that weren't going to be aimed at what's effectively the shonen audience in the West.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T13:25:44-07:00","id":1534302,"title":"","content":"Yes the 1990's was a mixed bag for superhero comics for both DC &amp; Marvel with Hal Jordon going mad and being replaced by Kyle Raynor in a span of four issues or less that even great writers like Kurt Busiek, Peter David, Grant Morrison or even cranky Mark Waid would have a hard time doing properly. The Spider-Clone saga and Marvel having to declare Chapter 11 bankruptcy and meaningless crossovers that just annoyed fans in general. The only titles I liked from DC was Starman by Will Robsinson, JLA by Grant Morrsion or Nightwing by Chuck Dixon from that time.<br /> <br /> I mostly go towards Manga now and more of the adult orientated stuff from Image that doesn't bother with superhero genre.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T15:54:13-07:00","id":1534308,"title":"","content":"As an aside, one resource I liked is Mechademia from Minnesota University. It's an academic journal all about anime and manga. There are original articles but a lot are translations of Japanese academic work. And it's very interesting. Especially the periodization-stuff. Interesting to look back on anime and the interaction between the broader culture and Otakus and whatnot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale","name":"Knightingale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27535_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-17T13:17:10-07:00","id":1534366,"title":"","content":"A side note that also around the 1990's we had the book called When Titans Clash and it went into great detail on why Marvel Comics went into Chapter 11 bankruptcy and it made for sober reading on how a number of bad choices can undo a company so easily.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-23T13:15:58-07:00","id":1534604,"title":"","content":"I recently picked up 